<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xuanzang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">602–664</span> · Also known as Hsuan-tsang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xuanzang was a Chinese Buddhist monk, scholar and traveller who journeyed overland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between 629 and 645 to collect scriptures. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a>, visited the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>, and on his return translated many texts into Chinese. His account, the Great Tang Records on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a>, is a major source for the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and India in the 7th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="Xuanzang" width="320" height="716" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xuanzang_w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
